WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:07.140
When I was in university, I hated Python. But&nbsp;now&nbsp;
that I'm in my career, I absolutely love it. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:13.620
video are the top reasons why devs like&nbsp;me and&nbsp;
many others love Python. First one is that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:19.920
is so easy to learn. And when I started&nbsp;learning&nbsp;
Python, I was in university and was learning it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:24.960
informally because I was assigned&nbsp;some work that&nbsp;
required Jupyter notebooks. At the time I hated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:31.680
it because I was forced to use&nbsp;it and I remember&nbsp;
being particularly annoyed by the fact that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:37.740
used the word "none" instead of "null". However,&nbsp;
I was really versed with Java and C at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:43.620
and so when I got more and more used to&nbsp;it, it&nbsp;
started feeling like executable pseudocode. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:49.260
is mainly because Python is a typeless&nbsp;language&nbsp;
and it's also an interpreted language.&nbsp;And what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:55.740
this means is basically that Python&nbsp;cuts a lot of&nbsp;
corners and handles a lot of things for you. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.740 --> 00:01:00.660
so for developers, where&nbsp;you would typically&nbsp;
get compile-time errors,&nbsp;you're going to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:05.580
runtime errors with Python.&nbsp;And so you just need&nbsp;
to be cognizant of that.&nbsp;But everyone deals with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:11.880
that. And so that&nbsp;leads us into reason two, the&nbsp;
community.&nbsp;There's a huge community for Python.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:18.120
It has been&nbsp;around for nearly 30 years now.&nbsp;
And so with that time there has been so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:22.560
tutorials and forums&nbsp;created. No matter what&nbsp;
you're dealing with,&nbsp;whether you're learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.340
Python or dealing&nbsp;with a certain concept in&nbsp;
your code,&nbsp;there's probably a tutorial that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:30.120
walks&nbsp;you through it or a forum where&nbsp;
hundreds of people have asked the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:34.260
question before. And then on top of that, if&nbsp;
you have a problem that no one has asked yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:39.660
you can ask&nbsp;it and someone will probably get&nbsp;
back to you. So the community is great. But next,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:46.920
we&nbsp;love that Python has a virtual environment.&nbsp;
And what virtual environments allow is for&nbsp;devs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:53.460
to encapsulate all of their scripts&nbsp;and libraries&nbsp;
into one portable environment. At IBM, I've used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:58.380
Python many different times for many different&nbsp;
projects, and with each project&nbsp;it has its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:03.960
own virtual environment. This way I could switch&nbsp;
between the two environments and not&nbsp;have to worry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:09.420
about the different libraries and dependencies&nbsp;
that go with each. Now, in a previous&nbsp;video I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:15.720
talked about cloud migration and how we used Python&nbsp;
scripts to analyze performance metrics. With that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:21.540
we created a virtual environment for that project&nbsp;
and I encapsulated it and shipped&nbsp;it off to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:27.060
rest of my teammates. This way we all have a&nbsp;
consistent environment to run the&nbsp;code and can get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:34.440
consistent answers. Lastly, us devs love Python&nbsp;
for its versatility. Now Python&nbsp;is a general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:40.620
purpose language and so it can be used across&nbsp;
many different platforms, everything&nbsp;from your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:45.900
mobile devices to laptops to servers.&nbsp;Python&nbsp;
has got you covered. And with Python&nbsp;being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:50.760
versatile and having a huge community, there's&nbsp;
libraries for nearly anything you&nbsp;can think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:56.940
of. I've personally used Python to develop some&nbsp;
natural language processing, some&nbsp;AI development,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:06.000
some numerical analysis, and even automated stock&nbsp;
trading. So with IBM-- er sorry --with Python&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:11.820
being across different platforms, having so many&nbsp;
different libraries and a great&nbsp;community, there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:16.680
so much you can do with it. Now, Python's not&nbsp;
going to be great when you&nbsp;need to tell a computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:23.340
where to put those 1's and 0's. However, for the&nbsp;
vast majority&nbsp;of other things that you need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:28.320
deal with, Python has got you covered. Thanks for&nbsp;
watching.&nbsp;If you like this content, be sure to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:32.160
like and subscribe. If you're new to Python and&nbsp;
want&nbsp;to learn more, check out the links below.

